<Layout title="Policy Divergence Model: Rules & Limitations" description="TRADE90's experimental policy-gap research: inflation objectives, underlying prices, employment, growth and official guidance. Read the rules and limitations." canonical="/policy-model/">
<main class="wrap"><article>
<p>EXPERIMENTAL RESEARCH · VERSION {MODEL_VERSION}</p><h1>Policy Divergence Model</h1>
<p>TRADE90 v3 compares market-implied 3-month policy levels, independent economic pressure, source-reviewed central-bank guidance and the resulting absolute policy-rate gap. Three calendar months are an approximation to a 90-day horizon. Market-implied future policy rates are data-provider expectations, not predictions of what banks must do. If licensed data is unavailable, the primary 90-day classification remains uncertain while separately reviewed qualitative policy candidates may still be described.</p>
<h2>Four-stage divergence workflow</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Market-implied policy path:</strong> only a provider-verified and publicly redistributable 3M projected policy level for both currencies may establish a quantitative rate-gap direction. OIS par rates, bond yields, vote summaries, forecasts, trading signals and unsupported survey values are not substitutes. Whole-snapshot freshness is limited to 96 hours, with no more than 15 minutes of observation mismatch between currencies. Without a usable market path, the directional 90-day composite abstains.</li>
<li><strong>Macroeconomic assessment:</strong> source-dated headline/underlying inflation, unemployment and GDP generate descriptive pressure scores for each bank. Those are context and never converted mechanically to expected basis-point moves.</li>
<li><strong>Central-bank evidence:</strong> the official policy statement and its interpretation must be source-bound, within the review window and not superseded by a later decision. An outdated review does not manufacture a direction or suppress a separately valid 3M market-implied observation.</li>
<li><strong>Compare signed and absolute rate gaps:</strong> expected implied policy levels give a signed future gap; its magnitude is compared with today's absolute benchmark difference. An explicitly uncalibrated five-basis-point materiality band withholds very small changes. A crossing through zero is disclosed; the absolute gap can widen or narrow even if signs reverse. If both reviewed policy biases actively contradict market pricing, the combined outcome remains uncertain.</li>
</ol>
<p>Directional results supported only by market pricing are marked as market-implied, not independently confirmed or calibrated. Results are never currency-price predictions, probabilities, or instructions to trade.</p>
<h2>Signed and absolute rate gaps</h2><p>The signed gap is the first currency’s selected policy rate minus the second currency’s rate. The absolute gap is its magnitude, regardless of sign. Divergence classifications describe this policy-rate difference, never the FX price direction. Widening and narrowing use neutral research styling, not trading-signal colours.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Current signed gap</th><th>Absolute gap outlook</th><th>Signed movement</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Positive</td><td>Widening</td><td>More positive</td><td>Base-minus-quote gap rises</td></tr><tr><td>Positive</td><td>Narrowing</td><td>Less positive</td><td>Base-minus-quote gap falls</td></tr><tr><td>Negative</td><td>Widening</td><td>More negative</td><td>Red ↓</td></tr><tr><td>Negative</td><td>Narrowing</td><td>Less negative</td><td>Green ↑</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p>At equal rates, different bank biases support possible divergence in the corresponding direction. Any narrowing outlook carries crossing risk: the higher-rate currency could reverse. We do not estimate move sizes, so this is an initial directional bias, not a guaranteed absolute gap at day 90.</p>
<h2>Inputs and policy objectives</h2><p>US policy inflation uses BEA headline PCE and core PCE, retrieved through FRED and transformed from price indices into year-on-year changes. Australia uses monthly headline CPI in the model while the comparison charts retain their quarterly history. Supporting underlying inflation comes from OECD CPI excluding food and energy for Australia, Switzerland, Canada, the UK, Japan and New Zealand, and Eurostat HICP excluding energy, food, alcohol and tobacco for the euro area (21 countries).</p>
<p>These underlying measures are not interchangeable with each bank’s preferred measures: Australian exclusion-based CPI is not trimmed-mean CPI; Canadian exclusion-based CPI is not CPI-trim/CPI-median; Japan’s exclusion measure differs from CPI excluding fresh food. Their purpose here is to check headline persistence, not claim a full representation of bank analysis.</p>
<ul>{Object.entries(policyFrameworks).map(([c,f])=><li><strong>{c}:</strong> {f.description} — <a href={f.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official framework</a></li>)}</ul>
<h2>Exact economic-pressure rules</h2><p>All thresholds below are model design assumptions, not calibrated coefficients or official policy rules. An objective is medium-term context, not an automatic trigger.</p><ul>
<li>For point objectives, the model uses a ±0.25 percentage-point buffer around 2%; this is not an official tolerance range. Australia uses 2–3%; Switzerland uses non-negative inflation below 2%. Canada and New Zealand retain their 2% focus for model comparisons; their official ranges remain visible above.</li>
<li>Headline and underlying inflation trends compare the latest observation with three months earlier, or six months for quarterly data. A ±0.3 percentage-point move defines a rising/falling trend. Decimal comparisons are rounded to eight places before threshold tests.</li>
<li>The single inflation pillar receives +2 when headline inflation is above the model reference zone, neither headline nor underlying inflation is falling materially, and underlying inflation exceeds the objective’s reference (2%; Australia 2.5%; Switzerland 1% as a model reference, not an SNB target). It receives −2 for headline below the zone with non-rising headline/underlying inflation and underlying below the reference. Otherwise, both inflation measures falling gives −1; other combinations give 0. Headline and core are not counted as two independent votes.</li>
<li>Unemployment compares three months earlier, or six months for quarterly data. A fall of at least 0.2 percentage points contributes +1; a rise contributes −1. An increase of at least 0.5 is a material weakness flag.</li>
<li>Two negative GDP quarters contribute −1. Two positive quarters exceeding that economy’s median of up to 20 earlier quarters contribute +1, requiring at least 12 historical quarters. The comparison excludes the two assessed quarters. Otherwise GDP contributes 0. This is historical context, not an estimate of potential output.</li>
<li>The three pillars sum to a descriptive pressure score. Positive inflation pressure and a total of at least +1 are labelled tightening pressure. Negative inflation pressure and a total of at most −1 are easing pressure. Other combinations are balanced or conflicting. The score does not estimate a rate change.</li>
</ul>
<h2>From economic pressure to policy outlook</h2><p>Each official-statement interpretation has its own publication date, source URL, review date and rationale. It must match the statement it interprets and cannot outlive a newer policy decision. A statement saying that the current meeting held rates is not forward hold guidance.</p><ul>
<li>Explicit conditional tightening guidance supports “Hike or hold” when inflation pressure and the total score are non-negative, without a material unemployment rise or two-quarter contraction.</li>
<li>Explicit conditional easing guidance supports “Cut or hold” when inflation pressure and the total score are non-positive.</li>
<li>Without directional guidance, a +2 inflation pillar and a score of at least +3 support “Hike or hold”, subject to the activity safeguards. A negative inflation pillar and score of at most −3 support “Cut or hold”.</li>
<li>“Conditional hold” requires an explicit reviewed policy-context rationale, headline and underlying readings within the reference zone, a score between −1 and +1, and no material activity weakness. The SNB’s unchanged-rate conditional forecast is contextual support, not a promise.</li>
<li>Everything else is uncertain. One minor opposing indicator is visible but does not automatically cancel strong evidence. Invalid economic inputs withhold the policy outlook. Expired or superseded policy commentary preserves validated macro evidence but labels the policy direction uncertain.</li>
</ul>
<h2>From bank outlooks to pair research</h2><p>Hike-or-hold, hold and cut-or-hold biases are compared in that order. Different biases support an initial signed-gap direction; the existing sign determines whether this means widening or narrowing. The gap can remain unchanged if both banks hold. Identical directional biases remain uncertain because move sizes and timing are unknown. Two supported hold baselines produce a broadly unchanged bias. Unknown policy direction on either side blocks a directional pair result.</p>
<h2>Freshness, automation and missing information</h2><p>Required series must have a successful retrieval within seven days, ordered finite observations and matching comparison periods. Monthly observations expire after 100 days; quarterly observations after 210 days measured from the end of their calendar quarter, not the first-day quarter label. UK unemployment uses the end of its rolling period. These are conservative maximum ages, not a release-calendar guarantee. Failed refreshes remain invalid even when cached points exist.</p><p>Guidance expires 90 days after publication or 14 days after its individual interpretation review; newer decisions invalidate earlier guidance. Policy snapshots expire after 45 days. A displayed pair expires at the earliest relevant input/guidance deadline or 48 hours after model generation. Rebuilding HTML alone does not refresh economic evidence or extend an assessment's validity.</p><p>Additional inputs refresh with the daily macro workflow. Publication discovery and supported numerical policy updates are automated. Interpretation of new statements still needs a reviewed, source-bound annotation; this version does not claim autonomous policy interpretation. New sources failing checks cannot silently replace verified data.</p><p>Geopolitical headlines and risk-on/risk-off sentiment are not scored directly. Their expected economic consequences can enter through official guidance before they appear in released data. Wage histories, complete bank forecast paths, financial-condition measures and release-surprise data are not included.</p>
<h2>Additional policy drivers under evaluation</h2>
<p>More indicators could improve context, but adding uncalibrated signals must not create numerical probabilities or artificial precision. The independent economic-pressure pillar does <strong>not</strong> score the following dimensions. They require verified source histories, release-date tracking and genuine out-of-sample validation:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wages and services inflation:</strong> indicators of domestic inflation persistence, including preferred measures that vary by bank.</li>
<li><strong>Inflation expectations:</strong> consumer, business and professional expectations where official time series are available.</li>
<li><strong>Policy projections and voting:</strong> bank forecasts, member votes, minutes and published conditional rate paths, with decision-time provenance.</li>
<li><strong>Growth and demand nowcasts:</strong> retail spending, PMIs, vacancy data and other early signals; these can be noisy and must not duplicate GDP and labour pillars.</li>
<li><strong>Financial conditions and credit:</strong> credit growth, lending conditions and exchange-rate effects where the bank considers them material.</li>
<li><strong>Market-implied policy rates:</strong> verified, licensed, policy-level 3M paths enter the separate expectations pillar (not the economic score), while unavailable data leaves the composite direction unresolved.</li>
<li><strong>Fiscal, commodity and geopolitical shocks:</strong> interpret through verifiable economic channels instead of ungrounded headline sentiment.</li>
</ul>
<p>New inputs should remain explanatory and unscored until tests using information available at each historical decision show incremental forecasting benefit. Published probability estimates require calibration and measurement of forecast error.</p>
<h2>Validation and version history</h2><p>Version 3.0.0 adds the licensed market-path / fundamentals / central-bank evidence / absolute-gap research workflow, with a fail-closed 3M feed, source-backed forecasts only and separate qualitative candidates. Version 2.0.3 validates exact observation dates, separates macro-score contrasts from forecasts, and explains unavailable versus uncertain states. Version 2.0.2 distinguishes stale editorial guidance from invalid economic data. Version 1.0.0 used headline trends, fixed GDP thresholds and neutral-to-hold conversion. Version 2.0.1 corrects quarterly input freshness and expiry to use the period end and guards nonstandard quarterly dates. Version 2.0.0 fixed decimal boundaries, adds underlying inflation and US PCE, incorporates policy objectives and historical growth context, separates economic pressure from policy bias, corrects signed-gap colours and requires supported hold context.</p><p>Forecast accuracy is not established. Tests verify calculation and safeguards, not economic predictive skill. The archive preserves earlier versions and records revised evidence, source/status changes and classifications. Identical successful polling alone does not create a new research assessment.</p><p>Evaluate saved outlooks prospectively against realised policy gaps and a no-change baseline. Include coverage, abstentions, crossing cases and forecast dependence. Revised historical observations can introduce hindsight bias; they must not be treated as information that was available at the original forecast time. Added inputs do not by themselves demonstrate improved accuracy.</p>
